        <h2 style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.6rem); font-weight: 700; color: #0A0A0A; margin: 2.4em 0 0.6em; padding-top: 0.4em;">DEET Aerosol Repellent vs Picaridin Aerosol Spray &amp; IR3535 Performance</h2>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">Formulators select among four primary active chemical classes registered for personal protection applications. Each active exhibits unique physical properties, toxicity profiles, solvency traits, and sensory characteristics on the skin. Selecting the right active dictates solvent choice, aerosol valve selection, and packaging compatibility.</p>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">DEET (N,N-Diethyl-meta-toluamide) remains the gold standard synthetic repellent introduced in the 1950s. A yellow-tinted liquid aromatic amide, DEET offers unmatched broad-spectrum protection against mosquitoes, ticks, chiggers, and biting flies. However, DEET acts as a powerful organic solvent that plasticizes synthetic polymers, acrylic watch crystals, synthetic garments, and painted surfaces. This physical limitation led to the development of modern alternatives like Picaridin and IR3535.</p>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">Picaridin aerosol spray formulations utilize hydroxyethyl isobutyl piperidine carboxylate (Icaridin). Picaridin provides vector protection equal or superior to DEET against Aedes, Anopheles, and Culex species without dissolving plastics or synthetic gear. It is non-greasy, virtually odorless, and exhibits low dermal absorption, making it an increasingly popular choice for premium cosmetic aerosol formulations. IR3535 (Ethyl butylacetylaminopropionate) represents a synthetic amino acid derivative offering excellent safety profiles, widely favored in European personal care markets for family and sensitive-skin repellents.</p>

