      <!-- ── SECTION 3 ── -->
      <section id="section-3">
        <h2 style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', Georgia, serif; font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.6rem); font-weight: 700; color: #0A0A0A; margin: 2.4em 0 0.6em; padding-top: 0.4em;">Polymer Plasticization &amp; Solvent Carrier Solvency Dynamics</h2>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">A major technical hurdle when formulating a DEET aerosol repellent is controlling its aggressive solvency toward synthetic materials. As a liquid aromatic amide with a high Hansen solubility parameter match for polar polymers, DEET readily penetrates polycarbonate eye protection, nylon webbing, rayon clothing, leather finishes, and polyurethane coatings, causing swelling, softening, and permanent surface clouding.</p>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">According to chemical data hosted on <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem</a>, DEET's solvent power stems from its dual polar carbonyl oxygen and hydrophobic aromatic ring. Formulators addressing plasticizer damage often explore natural insect repellent aerosol alternatives like p-Menthane-3,8-diol (PMD) or transition to Picaridin systems. When DEET cannot be substituted due to military or high-intensity vector protection specs, solvency is managed by blending high-flash isoparaffin solvents or silicones that buffer active contact with sensitive substrates.</p>

        <div style="border-left: 3px solid #16A34A; background: #F0FDF4; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; margin: 1.8em 0; font-size: 0.93rem;">
          <strong style="color: #16A34A; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Key Insight</strong>
          Formulating Picaridin or IR3535 in ethanol-water solvent bases achieves rapid skin drying without polymer attack; however, incorporating volatile cyclomethicones (such as cyclopentasiloxane) reduces dermal tackiness and lowers surface tension for flawless aerosol mist distribution.
        </div>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.4em;">In addition to consumer material safety, active solvency directly affects internal canister integrity. High concentrations of aromatic actives can permeate standard internal can linings or degrade valve stem elastomers over time. For more on commercial scale manufacturing considerations, read our overview of <a href="/cosmetic-aerosol-manufacturing" target="_top">Cosmetic Aerosol Manufacturing Practice</a>.</p>
      </section>

